Under [IRS](https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-simple-ira-contribution-limits) rules, an employer sponsoring a SIMPLE IRA must contribute every year, choosing between a dollar-for-dollar match up to 3% or a flat 2% nonelective contribution for every eligible employee whether they contribute or not. The employer may lower that match to no less than 1% in no more than two of five years, provided they give advance notice to employees. In practice, this reduction offers a limited way to ease a lean year without letting the employer skip the contribution.

A standard 401(k) gives a small-business employer far more cash-flow flexibility because employer contributions are entirely optional. While a safe harbor 401(k) requires a set employer contribution, a standard 401(k) never requires the business to match employee deferrals. As a result, a 401(k) employer can skip a match in a lean year, while a SIMPLE IRA employer has to contribute something every year.